// ============================================================================
// Project 25 (Meridian Pay) -- PREPARED Session 5, TO BE RUN Session 6
// 12_alert_grading.kql                                     [NEW -- 2026-08-18]
//
// Grades every row in `alerts` twice, independently:
//   gt_episode_id      -- the injected ground_truth episode this alert actually
//                         hit, or '' if the alert is a false positive
//   dispute_confirmed  -- whether a real chargeback landed at the same terminal
//                         inside the alert's own window
//
// This is what turns the project's pitch from a claim into a number. Contract
// Sec7: "Reflex precision measured against ground_truth AS WELL AS against
// disputes" -- two independent graders, because either one alone can be argued
// with. Ground truth says "we planted it and you found it"; disputes say "a real
// customer complained." An alert that satisfies both is not a coincidence.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY TWO COLUMNS ON `alerts` AND NOT A NEW TABLE.
// Contract Sec3 commits to 12 Eventhouse KQL tables as a number a reviewer counts.
// A 13th grading table would change that number, and per the contract's own rule
// that gets changed on purpose or not at all -- this decides not at all. Adding
// COLUMNS to an existing table is not adding a table, and there is direct
// precedent: `maturity_cohort` was added to this same table by
// 11_activator_reflex_alerts.kql Step 0 with the same additive .alter-merge.
// Table count stays 12.
//
// The Direct Lake model also wants it this way. Direct Lake supports no
// calculated columns, so any grading done in DAX would have to be a measure
// doing interval-overlap logic across a disconnected table on every visual
// refresh. Two boolean/string columns make every precision measure a plain
// COUNTROWS -- which is the shape Direct Lake is fast at.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY STAMPING THESE IS SAFE, WHEN STAMPING maturity_cohort WAS NOT.
// 01b_dispute_curated_FIX.kql established the rule: do not stamp a value that is
// a function of elapsed time, because a stored column cannot go stale loudly.
// That rule is respected here, not ignored:
//   * gt_episode_id is IMMUTABLE. Whether an alert's window overlapped a planted
//     episode's window is a fact about two fixed intervals. It cannot change.
//   * dispute_confirmed IS time-dependent -- more disputes mature every day. It
//     is therefore recomputed by a deliberate REGRADE step, not written once at
//     ingest by a policy that never re-runs. That distinction is the whole of
//     01b's lesson. Re-run this file before any precision read.
// maturity_cohort itself is still NOT recomputed here; it stays as written, and
// fn_dispute_graded()'s query-time cohort_now remains the authority for disputes.
//
// RUN ONE STATEMENT AT A TIME.
// ============================================================================


// ---- STEP 0: BACK UP FIRST. `alerts` holds the E2 evidence. ----------------
// Step 4 is a .set-or-replace. If its query is wrong, 186 evidenced rows -- the
// artefact export checkpoint E2 was closed on -- are gone. A working copy costs
// nothing and is dropped in step 7. Do not skip this to save a click.

.set alerts_pregrade_backup <| alerts

// Confirm the backup took, BEFORE touching the original. Must return 186.
alerts_pregrade_backup | count


// ---- STEP 1: add the two columns, additively -------------------------------

.alter-merge table alerts (gt_episode_id: string, dispute_confirmed: bool)

.show table alerts schema as json
// Confirm 15 columns: the 12 original + maturity_cohort + these 2.
//
// NOTE, corrected here rather than carried forward: 11_activator_reflex_alerts.kql
// Step 0's comment says "11 columns total (10 original + 1)", and HANDOVER.md
// repeated it. The original CREATE in 00_raw_and_staging_tables.kql defines 12
// columns (alert_id, fired_at, reflex_type, entity_type, entity_id, merchant_id,
// store_id, window_start, window_end, signal_value, threshold_value, evidence),
// so the count after maturity_cohort was 13, not 11. The .alter-merge itself was
// correct; only the count in the comment was wrong. Read the schema, not the note.


// ---- STEP 2: the grading function ----------------------------------------
//
// >>> HOW TO RUN THIS ONE. Do NOT click inside it and press Run. <<<
// SELECT THE WHOLE FUNCTION BY HAND -- from the `.create-or-alter` line down to
// and including the closing `}` -- then press Run.
//
// WHY. The first attempt failed with "Syntax error: I could not parse that,
// sorry." and the editor had highlighted only the first TWO lines. That is the
// diagnosis, not a mystery: the Queryset's auto-block detection ends a statement
// at the first `;`, and the first `;` here is the `let ref_time = ...;` INSIDE the
// body. It submitted an opening brace with no closing brace, which is exactly what
// an unbalanced-parse error looks like. Nothing was wrong with the KQL.
// This is the same class of problem Session 3 recorded ("I can't run the query at
// once, but it works if I select a certain statement and run individually") -- any
// multi-`let` body has to be selected manually in this editor.
//
// Counted-function note: this is infrastructure, NOT a 13th counted function. The
// counted 12 are unchanged (fn_reflex1-4, fn_dq_rule1-6, fn_dispute_graded,
// fn_issuers_for_estate_selection). fn_alert_grading sits with fn_build_dim_estate
// and the fn_transform_* policies as a build helper -- it populates a table; it is
// not something a dashboard, Activator rule or report calls.
//
// The body below was also hardened after that first failure, so that a second
// attempt fails for at most one reason. Three constructs were replaced with
// longer but unambiguous equivalents, none of which changes the result:
//   * `take_anyif(episode_id, hit)` -> `max(iff(hit, episode_id, ''))`. take_anyif
//     picks an arbitrary matching row; max over strings is deterministic, and an
//     unmatched row contributes '' which loses to any real episode_id. Determinism
//     matters here because this value is written to a table and compared later.
//   * `d_time between (window_start .. window_end)` -> explicit >= and <=.
//   * `on reflex_key, entity_key` -> explicit `$left.x == $right.x` pairs.
// The interior explanatory comments were moved up here as well, so the block you
// have to select by hand is as short as it can be.
//
// WHAT IT DOES. Two independent graders, per contract Sec7:
//   gt_episode_id     -- the injected ground_truth episode this alert actually hit,
//                        or '' if the alert is a false positive. An alert window and
//                        an episode window overlap iff EACH starts before the other
//                        ends; writing only half of that test is the classic
//                        interval bug and it fails quietly, matching too few.
//   dispute_confirmed -- whether a real chargeback landed at the same terminal
//                        inside the alert's own window.
// dispute_curated is deliberately the LEFT side of its join: ~6.7k rows against
// auth_curated's 5.39m, and Session 3 already hit LowMemoryCondition on a 5.38m-row
// operation. Small side left, narrow projection, no arg_max.
// Exactly one name differs between the two vocabularies -- reflex_type
// 'terminal_dark' is episode_type 'terminal_dark_outage' -- and it is mapped in ONE
// place, below, so the mismatch cannot become a silent zero in three consumers.

.create-or-alter function fn_alert_grading(as_of: datetime = datetime(null)) {
    let ref_time = iff(isnull(as_of), now(), as_of);
    let gt = materialize(
        ground_truth
        | extend reflex_key = iff(episode_type == 'terminal_dark_outage', 'terminal_dark', episode_type)
        | project episode_id, reflex_key, entity_key = affected_entity_id, gt_start = window_start, gt_end = window_end);
    let disputed = materialize(
        dispute_curated
        | where dispute_time <= ref_time
        | join kind=inner (auth_curated | where event_type == 'auth' | project auth_id, d_terminal = terminal_id, d_time = event_time) on auth_id
        | project dispute_id, d_terminal, d_time);
    let a = materialize(alerts | extend reflex_key = reflex_type, entity_key = entity_id);
    let gt_hit = a
        | join kind=leftouter (gt) on $left.reflex_key == $right.reflex_key, $left.entity_key == $right.entity_key
        | extend hit = isnotempty(episode_id) and gt_start <= window_end and gt_end >= window_start
        | summarize matched = max(iff(hit, episode_id, '')) by alert_id;
    let d_hit = a
        | join kind=leftouter (disputed) on $left.entity_key == $right.d_terminal
        | extend hit = isnotempty(dispute_id) and d_time >= window_start and d_time <= window_end
        | summarize dispute_hits = countif(hit) by alert_id;
    a
    | join kind=leftouter (gt_hit) on alert_id
    | join kind=leftouter (d_hit) on alert_id
    | extend gt_episode_id = coalesce(matched, ''), dispute_confirmed = coalesce(dispute_hits, 0) > 0
    | project alert_id, fired_at, reflex_type, entity_type, entity_id, merchant_id, store_id, window_start, window_end, signal_value, threshold_value, evidence, maturity_cohort, gt_episode_id, dispute_confirmed
}

// >>> END OF THE BLOCK TO SELECT. <<<
// Confirm it took before moving on -- a function that failed to create makes every
// step below fail with a different, more confusing error:
//   .show functions | where Name == 'fn_alert_grading'

// ---- STEP 3: DRY RUN. Read the grading before writing it. ------------------
// "The command didn't error" is not proof. Read these three, in order, and only
// continue if all three are sane.

fn_alert_grading(datetime(2026-08-17)) | count
// FLOOR: must be exactly 186. Anything less means a join dropped rows and the
// .set-or-replace in step 4 would silently delete evidence. Anything more means
// a join fanned out -- most likely an alert overlapping two episodes, which
// take_anyif should have collapsed. Either way: STOP and diagnose.

fn_alert_grading(datetime(2026-08-17))
| summarize alerts = count(),
            gt_matched = countif(gt_episode_id != ''),
            dispute_matched = countif(dispute_confirmed)
    by reflex_type
| extend gt_precision = round(todouble(gt_matched) / todouble(alerts), 3)
| order by reflex_type asc
// EXPECTATIONS, written down BEFORE running so the result can disagree with them:
//   * terminal_dark -- expect gt_precision at or near 1.000. Session 4 filtered
//     these to the 9 ground-truth-matched rows before writing them, so anything
//     below 1.0 means this grader disagrees with that filter and one of the two
//     is wrong.
//   * card_testing_burst -- expect well below 1.0. 72 alerts against 40 planted
//     episodes; many are multiple 5-minute buckets inside one episode (which
//     count as matched), but the detector's deliberate margin under the injection
//     parameters means genuine false positives are expected and are not a defect.
//   * issuer_degradation -- 95 alerts against 12 planted episodes. Low precision
//     here is the interesting result, not a bug: series_decompose_anomalies flags
//     any hour outside 2.5 sigma of an issuer's own baseline, and real traffic has
//     more such hours than we planted.
//   * dispute_matched -- expect LOW everywhere, and near zero for terminal_dark.
//     A dead terminal generates no transactions, so it cannot generate
//     chargebacks. That is the correct answer, and it is exactly why grading
//     against disputes ALONE would be misleading and the contract asks for both.

fn_alert_grading(datetime(2026-08-17))
| where gt_episode_id != ''
| summarize episodes_detected = dcount(gt_episode_id) by reflex_type
// Recall's numerator. Compare against ground_truth's own 40 / 25 / 12 / 60 split.


// ---- STEP 4: write it ------------------------------------------------------
// Only after step 3's floor returned exactly 186.

.set-or-replace alerts <| fn_alert_grading(datetime(2026-08-17))


// ---- STEP 5: read the write back -------------------------------------------

alerts | count
// Must still be 186. If this dropped, restore immediately from the backup:
//   .set-or-replace alerts <| alerts_pregrade_backup

alerts
| summarize firings = count(), graded = countif(gt_episode_id != '')
    by reflex_type, maturity_cohort
| order by reflex_type asc
// Cross-check against the Session 4 table in Gate_Check_Results.md:
//   card_testing_burst  graded_T90_T60 29 / partially_matured_T60_T30 18 /
//                       awaiting_grading_T30_T 25
//   issuer_degradation  awaiting_grading_T30_T 95
//   terminal_compromise awaiting_grading_T30_T 10
//   terminal_dark       awaiting_grading_T30_T  9
// The firings column MUST reproduce those six numbers exactly. maturity_cohort is
// passed through untouched by fn_alert_grading, so any change is a defect in the
// regrade, not new information.


// ---- STEP 6: the number the whole project exists to publish ----------------

alerts
| where maturity_cohort == 'graded_T90_T60'
| summarize alerts_raised = count(), true_positives = countif(gt_episode_id != '')
    by reflex_type
| extend precision = round(todouble(true_positives) / todouble(alerts_raised), 3)
// ONLY the graded cohort. This is the one cohort where precision is a real
// number rather than a premature one -- which today means card_testing_burst
// alone, since Session 4's retried as_of values put the other three reflexes
// entirely in awaiting_grading_T30_T. Reporting a blended figure across all four
// is the exact defect contract Sec5 exists to prevent.


// ---- STEP 7: drop the backup, AFTER steps 5 and 6 both looked right --------
// Leave it in place if anything above surprised you. It costs nothing to keep
// and everything to have dropped too early.

.drop table alerts_pregrade_backup

// ============================================================================
// AFTER THIS FILE: the two new columns must reach the semantic model before the
// Alert Precision page can use them.
//   1. OneLake availability re-mirrors `alerts` automatically -- allow time, and
//      confirm Pending size is 0B on the Database details pane before assuming it.
//   2. The Lakehouse shortcut to `alerts` picks the new columns up, but the
//      SEMANTIC MODEL DOES NOT until the table is refreshed/re-added there.
//      Confirm both new columns are visible in SM_MeridianPay_DirectLake's field
//      list before running the measure read-back.
// Neither step is automatic. See 00_Plan/SESSION_05_PORTAL_STEPS.md Sec4.
